<!-- Generated by Pandoc using pretext.lua -->

	<subsection xml:id="forms-summary">
		<title>Forms Summary</title>

		<p>
			Forms are an essential part of most full-fledged web applications, because
			you’ll want to get more information from the user than you can get with just
			mouse clicks. Here is a quick reminder of what we’ve learned about HTML forms:
		</p>

		<p><ol>
			<li>
			The <c>form</c> tag surrounds the entire form; forms cannot be nested.
			</li>

			<li>
			The <c>form</c> tag has attributes of <c>method</c> and <c>action</c> that say where and how the data is submitted. We’ll discuss that elsewhere.
			</li>

			<li>
			The form comprises a bunch of inputs (technically called <q>controls</q>) to get data from the user.
			</li>

			<li>
			Each input should have a <c>name</c> attribute that you the programmer choose as part of designing the form. The corresponding <c>value</c> is either typed by the user or chosen from a set of possible values, as with <c>select</c> menus or radio buttons.
			</li>

			<li>
			Each input should be <em>labeled</em>, which associates a user-friendly description of the input with the input itself. This makes the form more <em>accessible</em> to all users.
			</li>

			<li>
			The <url href="https://wave.webaim.org/">WAVE</url> validator tool is an excellent way to check the accessibility of a form. The WAVE browser plug-in is highly recommended.
			</li>

			<li>
			The <c>input</c> tag has a bunch of useful types, like <c>text</c>, <c>password</c>, <c>number</c> and more. The <c>datetime</c> and <c>datetime-local</c> types bring up a date picker (the <c>datetime-local</c> omits the timezone)
			</li>

			<li>
			The <c>textarea</c> tag creates a larger box for long textual input.
			</li>

			<li>
			The <c>select</c> and <c>option</c> tags are used to create menus
			</li>

			<li>
			Create radio buttons with <c>&lt;input type=radio ...&gt;</c> and create checkboxes with <c>&lt;input type=checkbox ...&gt;</c> Each group of buttons/boxes should have a shared <c>name</c> attribute. Each input needs to be labeled, but the entire group is put into a <c>fieldset</c> container with a <c>legend</c> that describes the group.
			</li>

			<li>
			The form should have a <c>&lt;button type="submit"&gt;&lt;/button&gt;</c> in order to submit the data for processing.
			</li>

		</ol></p>

		<p>
			That list is mostly a quick reminder. See the various subsections for more
			detail.
		</p>
	</subsection>
